From ae42c27b5411ecb19a5145cec4e53e235910e624 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 21:52:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 30/31] windows: stop telling the guest its APIC timer runs at
 1 kHz

The first Linux boot on WHP came out with every one of its 78 console
lines carrying the same printk timestamp, [0.190255], across 1.3s of host
wall clock. That is the signature of a dead tick, not a dead clocksource:
printk timestamps come from local_clock(), and on the unstable-sched-clock
path sched_clock_local() clamps its result to scd->tick_gtod + TICK_NSEC.
tick_gtod is only advanced by sched_clock_tick(), which only runs from the
timer interrupt, so once the tick stops every later printk reports the
same value -- one that is deliberately not tick-aligned.

The tick stopped because of the crystal frequency in CPUID leaf 0x15.
native_calibrate_tsc() (arch/x86/kernel/tsc.c) reads the leaf once and
derives three things from it:

    crystal_khz        = ECX / 1000
    tsc_khz            = crystal_khz * EBX / EAX
    lapic_timer_period = crystal_khz * 1000 / HZ

Describing the crystal as 1 kHz (ECX=1000, EAX=1, EBX=tsc_hz/1000) makes
the second line exact -- which is why the host log looked right and the
guest's TSC really did run at the advertised rate -- and the third line
degenerate: crystal_khz truncates to 1 and lapic_timer_period becomes
1000/HZ, i.e. 4 counts at HZ=250. __setup_APIC_LVTT() then writes
lapic_timer_period / APIC_DIVISOR == 0 to the APIC initial-count register,
and a zero initial count stops the timer (SDM Vol. 3 11.5.4). No timer
interrupt ever arrives: no jiffies, no sched_clock_tick(), no hrtimers,
so every timeout and sleep in the guest hangs.

The KVM backend never meets this because KvmIoapic::new creates an
in-kernel i8254 and the guest calibrates against the PIT; hvf is aarch64
and uses the architected timer. WHP has no PIT, no HPET and no PM timer,
so CPUID is the guest's only source for both frequencies -- and the
"already calibrated" shortcut in calibrate_APIC_clock() is the path we
want it on, because the empirical fallback ends in a verification pass
that counts jiffies a nonexistent PIT never delivers.

So publish a real crystal frequency: ask WHP for its interrupt clock
(WHvCapabilityCodeInterruptClockFrequency), fall back to the 100 MHz that
crosvm's WHPX backend uses for a partition with WHP LAPIC emulation, and
build EBX/EAX as the ratio that reproduces the measured TSC frequency
against it. Linux computes crystal_khz * EBX in 32-bit arithmetic, so the
denominator is sized to keep that product under 2^32 -- the real
constraint, rather than the overflow in EBX alone the old comment worried
about.

UNVERIFIED: no Windows host was available. The arithmetic is tested
against a transcription of the kernel's own formula, but whether WHP's
emulated LAPIC really counts at the frequency it reports is undocumented,
which is why both the reported and the used value are logged.
